<rss version="2.0" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"><channel><title>智能新闻| smithsonianmag.com</title><link>https://www.smithsonianmag.com/rss/smart-news/</link><description>智能新闻的 RSS 提要</description><lastBuildDate>Thu, 22 Oct 2026 02:15:54 GMT</lastBuildDate><generator>rfeed v1.1.1</generator><docs>https://github.com/svpino/rfeed/blob/master/README.md</docs><item><title>考古学家可能已经解开了 1869 年在威斯敏斯特教堂出土的罗马石棺之谜</title><link>https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/archaeologists-may-have-solved-the-mystery-of-a-roman-sarcophagus-excavated-at-westminster-abbey-in-1869-180989732/</link><description>新的研究表明，这座古老的棺材在 10 世纪或 11 世纪被重新利用，用来存放一位曾监督伦敦修道院的方丈的遗骸</description><pubDate>Wed, 21 Oct 2026 20:34:09 GMT</pubDate><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/archaeologists-may-have-solved-the-mystery-of-a-roman-sarcophagus-excavated-at-westminster-abbey-in-1869-180989732/</guid></item><item><title>研究表明，非洲象塑造了稀树草原生态系统，并在死后很长一段时间内继续这样做</title><link>https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/african-elephants-shape-the-savanna-ecosystem-and-continue-doing-so-long-after-theyve-died-study-suggests-180989718/</link><description>起初，大象尸体下面的植物会死亡。土壤微生物和植物很快就会重新生长，但只有少数选定的物种。食草动物后来进来咀嚼营养丰富的蔬菜</description><pubDate>Wed, 21 Oct 2026 18:47:22 GMT</pubDate><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/african-elephants-shape-the-savanna-ecosystem-and-continue-doing-so-long-after-theyve-died-study-suggests-180989718/</guid></item><item><title>上海“大胆”的新歌剧院刚刚向公众开放，还有一个反重力楼梯</title><link>https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/shanghais-audacious-new-opera-house-just-opened-its-doors-and-a-gravity-defying-staircase-to-the-public-180989726/</link><description>滨江歌剧院是上海最新的文化宣言。它设有三个表演厅，旨在向所有人开放，而不仅仅是持票者</description><pubDate>Wed, 21 Oct 2026 17:50:54 GMT</pubDate><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/shanghais-audacious-new-opera-house-just-opened-its-doors-and-a-gravity-defying-staircase-to-the-public-180989726/</guid></item><item><title>照片中第一批站立的人类到底在哪里？三位痴迷的电影制片人解开了近 200 年前的谜团</title><link>https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/where-exactly-were-the-first-humans-to-be-captured-in-a-photograph-standing-three-obsessed-filmmakers-solved-the-nearly-200-year-old-mystery-180989727/</link><description>侦探们花了两年时间挖掘档案并构建 19 世纪巴黎的 3D 模型。现在，该地点因纪念该历史遗址的青铜牌匾和“诗意的”早期照片而永垂不朽</description><pubDate>Wed, 21 Oct 2026 16:29:02 GMT</pubDate><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/where-exactly-were-the-first-humans-to-be-captured-in-a-photograph-standing-three-obsessed-filmmakers-solved-the-nearly-200-year-old-mystery-180989727/</guid></item><item><title>大约 500 年前，她绘制了第一幅已知的艺术家工作自画像并签名。凯瑟琳娜·范·赫梅森是谁？</title><link>https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/she-painted-first-known-self-portrait-an-artist-at-work-and-signed-it-nearly-500-years-ago-who-was-catharina-van-hemessen-180989724/</link><description>16 世纪，很少有女性成为职业艺术家。比利时安特卫普举办的一个新展览旨在将这位佛兰德画家恢复到艺术史巨人的万神殿中</description><pubDate>Wed, 21 Oct 2026 16:01:03 GMT</pubDate><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/she-painted-first-known-self-portrait-an-artist-at-work-and-signed-it-nearly-500-years-ago-who-was-catharina-van-hemessen-180989724/</guid></item><item><title>这些蛇可以侧手翻远离危险。科学家们刚刚发现了这些滑行生物如何表演杂技般的翻滚动作</title><link>https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/these-snakes-can-cartwheel-away-from-danger-scientists-just-uncovered-how-the-slithering-creatures-perform-their-acrobatic-rolls-180989720/</link><description>一项新的研究表明，当它们滚动时，一些属于拟蛇属的蛇可以达到每小时 3.8 至 8.3 英里的最高速度</description><pubDate>Wed, 21 Oct 2026 16:00:00 GMT</pubDate><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/these-snakes-can-cartwheel-away-from-danger-scientists-just-uncovered-how-the-slithering-creatures-perform-their-acrobatic-rolls-180989720/</guid></item><item><title>通过显微镜观察，从神经元到圆珠笔，科学与世俗变得令人惊讶地眼花缭乱</title><link>https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/see-the-scientific-and-the-mundane-become-surprisingly-dazzling-when-viewed-through-a-microscope-from-neurons-to-ballpoint-pens-180989721/</link><description>尼康小世界大赛公布了第 52 届年度大赛的摄影获奖者名单。几周前，其视频获胜者因使用生成人工智能而被废黜</description><pubDate>Wed, 21 Oct 2026 14:00:00 GMT</pubDate><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/see-the-scientific-and-the-mundane-become-surprisingly-dazzling-when-viewed-through-a-microscope-from-neurons-to-ballpoint-pens-180989721/</guid></item><item><title>天文学家首先发现了一颗可能发出绿光的恒星。新研究强调白矮星可能呈现的奇怪色调</title><link>https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/in-a-first-astronomers-identify-a-star-that-might-glow-green-new-research-highlights-the-strange-hues-white-dwarfs-can-take-on-180989719/</link><description>白矮星距离地球太远，无法直接观测到。因此，科学家根据它们发出的光的波长重建了它们在人眼中的外观，并发现了一系列可能呈现黄色、紫色或粉红色的彩色物体</description><pubDate>Wed, 21 Oct 2026 14:00:00 GMT</pubDate><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/in-a-first-astronomers-identify-a-star-that-might-glow-green-new-research-highlights-the-strange-hues-white-dwarfs-can-take-on-180989719/</guid></item><item><title>为了了解中世纪英格兰的社会鸿沟，考古学家检查了铅矿社区的牙釉质</title><link>https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/to-understand-social-divides-in-medieval-england-archaeologists-examined-tooth-enamel-from-lead-mining-communities-180989722/</link><description>13世纪峰区的一些居民可能比工业革命期间的城市居民经历了更严重的铅污染。女性骨骼的铅含量更高，死亡年龄更早</description><pubDate>Wed, 21 Oct 2026 11:00:00 GMT</pubDate><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/to-understand-social-divides-in-medieval-england-archaeologists-examined-tooth-enamel-from-lead-mining-communities-180989722/</guid></item><item><title>罗德岛州的一名男子种植了第一个重量超过 3,000 磅的南瓜。巨型葫芦是有史以来最大的水果</title><link>https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/a-rhode-island-man-grew-the-first-pumpkin-to-surpass-3000-pounds-the-gargantuan-gourd-is-the-largest-fruit-ever-180989723/</link><description>10 月 10 日，罗恩·华莱士 (Ron Wallace) 凭借 3,156 磅重的手枪摘得南瓜王桂冠。之前的纪录是英国种植者伊恩·佩顿 (Ian Paton) 在一周前创下的南瓜重近 2,857 磅的令人尊敬的纪录</description><pubDate>Tue, 20 Oct 2026 21:20:19 GMT</pubDate><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/a-rhode-island-man-grew-the-first-pumpkin-to-surpass-3000-pounds-the-gargantuan-gourd-is-the-largest-fruit-ever-180989723/</guid></item><item><title>生病的珊瑚可以在健康珊瑚的“神奇触摸”帮助下治愈，为受热破坏的海洋生态系统带来希望</title><link>https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/sick-corals-can-heal-with-the-help-of-a-magic-touch-from-healthy-ones-offering-hope-for-heat-damaged-marine-ecosystems-180989693/</link><description>珊瑚通常是一群基因相同的微小动物。新的研究表明，当白化的珊瑚碎片与色彩缤纷、繁盛的克隆体接触时，苍白的珊瑚可以在几周内复活</description><pubDate>Tue, 20 Oct 2026 18:56:20 GMT</pubDate><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/sick-corals-can-heal-with-the-help-of-a-magic-touch-from-healthy-ones-offering-hope-for-heat-damaged-marine-ecosystems-180989693/</guid></item><item><title>庞大的新数据库显示英国如何通过在本国附近贸易煤炭、葡萄酒和木材等商品来建立全球帝国</title><link>https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/a-vast-new-database-shows-how-britain-built-a-global-empire-by-trading-goods-like-coal-wine-and-wood-closer-to-home-180989717/</link><description>历史学家利用人工智能梳理了数十万页手写的港口记录，构建了英国海上贸易路线的交互式地图。即使英国在亚洲和美洲扩张，其与欧洲的贸易仍然势不可挡</description><pubDate>Tue, 20 Oct 2026 18:34:12 GMT</pubDate><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/a-vast-new-database-shows-how-britain-built-a-global-empire-by-trading-goods-like-coal-wine-and-wood-closer-to-home-180989717/</guid></item><item><title>世界上最高的树木在热浪期间似乎很难自己制造食物，随着世界变暖，它们变得脆弱</title><link>https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/the-worlds-tallest-trees-seem-to-struggle-to-make-their-own-food-during-heat-waves-leaving-them-vulnerable-as-the-world-warms-180989714/</link><description>一项新的研究表明，仅从加利福尼亚州中部到俄勒冈州南部生长的海岸红杉在气温升高时会减缓光合作用。科学家担心气候变化中这些高耸植物的未来</description><pubDate>Tue, 20 Oct 2026 16:00:00 GMT</pubDate><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/the-worlds-tallest-trees-seem-to-struggle-to-make-their-own-food-during-heat-waves-leaving-them-vulnerable-as-the-world-warms-180989714/</guid></item><item><title>这只狗学会了嗅出一种被称为“海呕吐物”的粘糊糊的海洋入侵者——这一技能为她赢得了年度动物奖</title><link>https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/this-dog-learned-to-sniff-out-a-slimy-marine-invader-called-sea-vomit-a-skill-that-earned-her-the-animal-of-the-year-award-180989706/</link><description>来自苏格兰高地的6岁拉布拉多犬Sgiath获得了国际动物福利基金会颁发的荣誉。据信，她是第一只接受训练来捕猎入侵性地毯海鞘的小狗，这种海鞘正在威胁英国海岸和其他地方的海洋生物。</description><pubDate>Tue, 20 Oct 2026 14:00:00 GMT</pubDate><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/this-dog-learned-to-sniff-out-a-slimy-marine-invader-called-sea-vomit-a-skill-that-earned-her-the-animal-of-the-year-award-180989706/</guid></item><item><title>考古学家在格陵兰岛寻找北欧教堂的证据，在红发埃里克的定居点附近发现了“铁证”</title><link>https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/archaeologists-searching-for-evidence-of-a-norse-church-in-greenland-have-found-the-smoking-gu-near-erik-the-reds-settlement-180989716/</link><description>几十年前，该地点首次被确定为可能的教堂，但研究人员直到今年夏天在那里发现了人类遗骸才证实了这一预感。它可能为 15 世纪维京人的不明失踪事件提供另一条线索</description><pubDate>Tue, 20 Oct 2026 11:00:00 GMT</pubDate><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/archaeologists-searching-for-evidence-of-a-norse-church-in-greenland-have-found-the-smoking-gu-near-erik-the-reds-settlement-180989716/</guid></item><item><title>法国市政厅的烧毁是一场“遗产灾难”，悲剧性地让人想起巴黎圣母院火灾</title><link>https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/the-burning-of-this-french-city-hall-was-a-catastrophe-for-heritage-tragically-reminiscent-of-the-notre-dame-fire-180989711/</link><description>法国当局表示，建于 19 世纪末、被誉为建筑瑰宝的利摩日市政厅在周末法国大规模抗议活动中成为纵火袭击的受害者</description><pubDate>Mon, 19 Oct 2026 21:13:51 GMT</pubDate><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/the-burning-of-this-french-city-hall-was-a-catastrophe-for-heritage-tragically-reminiscent-of-the-notre-dame-fire-180989711/</guid></item><item><title>科学家们不知道火星是如何获得它的两个卫星的。日本刚刚启动了一项历史性的太空任务，将采集样本以找出答案</title><link>https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/scientists-dont-know-how-mars-acquired-its-two-moons-japan-just-launched-a-historic-space-mission-that-will-fetch-samples-to-find-out-180989713/</link><description>日本宇宙航空研究开发机构的宇宙飞船预计将于 2027 年 8 月抵达火星系统，并于 2031 年将火卫一月球样本返回地球</description><pubDate>Mon, 19 Oct 2026 19:41:00 GMT</pubDate><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/scientists-dont-know-how-mars-acquired-its-two-moons-japan-just-launched-a-historic-space-mission-that-will-fetch-samples-to-find-out-180989713/</guid></item><item><title>当一位 19 世纪的苏格兰地主封锁一条公共人行道时，当地人发起了反抗。现在，考古学家出土了他备受争议的城墙的一部分</title><link>https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/when-a-19th-century-scottish-landowner-blocked-a-public-footpath-locals-rebelled-now-archaeologists-have-unearthed-parts-of-his-controversial-wall-180989712/</link><description>对“哈维堤”（一对建于 1820 年代的石墙）的反抗是格拉斯哥工人和中产阶级的胜利，他们利用骚乱、抵制和诉讼赢回了克莱德河的通行权</description><pubDate>Mon, 19 Oct 2026 19:17:45 GMT</pubDate><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/when-a-19th-century-scottish-landowner-blocked-a-public-footpath-locals-rebelled-now-archaeologists-have-unearthed-parts-of-his-controversial-wall-180989712/</guid></item><item><title>研究表明，地球真正的北极和南极在过去 3.2 亿年里已经漂移了四次</title><link>https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/earths-true-north-and-south-poles-have-wandered-four-times-over-the-past-320-million-years-study-suggests-180989698/</link><description>这种涉及地理极点的现象与磁极移动时不同。地球的固体地壳和熔融地幔偶尔会发生移动，随后在所谓的真正的极地漂移期间重新调整方向</description><pubDate>Mon, 19 Oct 2026 15:01:46 GMT</pubDate><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/earths-true-north-and-south-poles-have-wandered-four-times-over-the-past-320-million-years-study-suggests-180989698/</guid></item><item><title>科罗拉多州的野生动物减缓工作（包括北美最大的动物过境立交桥）如何让每个人都更安全</title><link>https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/how-colorados-wildlife-mitigation-efforts-including-the-largest-animal-crossing-overpass-in-north-america-are-making-everyone-safer-180989704/</link><description>得益于围栏、地下通道和大型立交桥等新基础设施，动物现在可以穿越 25 号州际公路的危险路段，这使得野生动物与车辆的碰撞事故减少了 91%</description><pubDate>Mon, 19 Oct 2026 14:00:00 GMT</pubDate><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/how-colorados-wildlife-mitigation-efforts-including-the-largest-animal-crossing-overpass-in-north-america-are-making-everyone-safer-180989704/</guid></item></channel></rss>